# Tessera stdlib algorithm concepts
#
# Concepts are buildtime required routine sets. They do not imply a runtime
# vtable by themselves. A generic routine names an Ops type and constrains it
# with one of these concepts.
#
# `routine Me.name` belongs to the concept's first parameter. A concept whose
# routines belong to another parameter names it (`routine TIter.emit`). A type
# satisfies a concept through a `conform` clause or declaration, checked against
# these signatures. HashEquatable, which only groups Equatable and Hashable, needs none.

module Standard/Core

### Types whose values can be compared for equality with `eq`.
### :typeparam T: The type compared for equality.
protocol Equatable[T]
needs T: typename
    ### True when `me` and `other` are equal.
    ### :param other: The value to compare with.
    ### :returns: True when the two values are equal.
    routine Me.eq(me: Me, other: Me) -> Bool

### Types that can be hashed to a U64 with `hash`. Values that are equal must hash the same.
### :typeparam T: The type that is hashed.
protocol Hashable[T]
needs T: typename
    ### The 64-bit hash of `me`.
    ### :returns: The 64-bit hash.
    routine Me.hash(me: Me) -> U64

### Types that are both Equatable and Hashable, as hash tables and sets require of their keys.
### :typeparam T: The key type.
protocol HashEquatable[T]
obeys Equatable[T], Hashable[T]
needs T: typename

### Types with a three-way ordering through `compare`, as sorting, the sorted collections, and `PriorityQueue` require.
### :typeparam T: The type being ordered.
protocol Comparable[T]
needs T: typename
    ### Compares `me` with `other`: -1 when `me` < `other`, 0 when they're equal, and 1 when `me` > `other`.
    ### :param other: The value to compare with.
    ### :returns: -1, 0, or 1.
    routine Me.compare(me: Me, other: Me) -> S32

### An iterator type `TIter` that hands out elements of type `TItem` one at a time: `emit` gives the element's value
### and crashes at the end, `emit_checked` gives `Unit` there.
### :typeparam TItem: The element type produced.
### :typeparam TIter: The iterator type.
protocol Iterator[TItem, TIter]
needs TItem: typename, TIter: typename
    ### Advances the iterator and returns the element it was on. It changes the iterator's state, so `me` is a pointer.
    ### The routine belongs to `TIter`, so it names its owner (`Me` in it is `TIter`).
    ### :returns: The next element, or `Unit` when the iterator is exhausted.
    routine TIter.emit_checked(me: @Me) -> Maybe[TItem]

    ### Advances the iterator and returns the element it was on.
    ### :returns: The next element.
    ### :throws: Crashes with `AbsentValueError` when the iterator is exhausted.
    routine TIter.emit(me: @Me) -> TItem

### An iterator type `TIter` over elements that live in memory, which also hands out each element's slot: `next`
### gives its address and crashes at the end, `next_checked` gives `Unit` there. An iterator that makes its values
### (a range, an adapter) has no slots and only meets `Iterator`.
### :typeparam TItem: The element type.
### :typeparam TIter: The iterator type.
protocol SlotIterator[TItem, TIter]
obeys Iterator[TItem, TIter]
needs TItem: typename, TIter: typename
    ### Advances the iterator and returns the slot of the element it was on.
    ### :returns: The next element's address, or `Unit` when the iterator is exhausted.
    routine TIter.next_checked(me: @Me) -> Maybe[@TItem]

    ### Advances the iterator and returns the slot of the element it was on.
    ### :returns: The next element's address.
    ### :throws: Crashes with `AbsentValueError` when the iterator is exhausted.
    routine TIter.next(me: @Me) -> @TItem

### A type that holds resources (memory, handles) and releases them in `destruct`. Collections of such values offer
### `destruct_all`, which destructs the elements too.
protocol Destructible[T]
needs T: typename
    ### Releases what the value holds and leaves it empty.
    routine Me.destruct(me: @Me) -> None
